      "title": "Vail, Ziolkowski, Beissel Heath selected for endowed faculty positions",
      "description": "Vail, Ziolkowski, Beissel Heath selected for endowed faculty positions at UNK – UNK NEWS Skip to content Saturday, September 5, 2026 UNK NEWS Power of the Herd Search Search Home Alumni Features Ask An Antelope Athletics Campus Calendar Commencement Chancellor Schnoor Student Life YOU tube video Contact Us Home 2026 August 25 Vail, Ziolkowski, Beissel Heath selected for endowed faculty positions at UNK College of Arts & Sciences Faculty Featured History News UNK Online Vail, Ziolkowski, Beissel Heath selected for endowed faculty positions at UNK August 25, 2026 Tyler Ellyson Post Views: 1,915 David Vail Theodora Ziolkowski Michelle Beissel Heath KEARNEY – Three University of Nebraska at Kearney faculty members have been selected for endowed positions recognizing their accomplishments in teaching, scholarship and service. David Vail, Theodora Ziolkowski and Michelle Beissel Heath, all faculty members in the College of Arts and Sciences, recently received endowed appointments supporting their continued academic and professional work. David Vail Vail, a professor in the Department of History , is the newest Ron and Carol Cope Professor. The five-year professorship recognizes faculty excellence and provides a $10,000 annual stipend. A UNK faculty member since 2016, Vail teaches undergraduate and graduate courses in areas such as environmental and agricultural history, U.S. history, public history and the history of science and medicine. His research focuses on many of those same areas, with an emphasis on the Great Plains. Vail has received numerous honors for his teaching, including the James C. Giesen Teaching Excellence Award in Agricultural and Rural History from the Agricultural History Society, Pratt-Heins Award for Teaching and UNK’s Excellence in Teaching General Studies Award. He was named UNK’s Outstanding Faculty Member of the Year by undergraduate students in 2020-21. A first-generation college graduate, Vail serves on the UNK First Generation Leadership Team and chairs the Humanities Nebraska Board of Directors. He has published extensively in academic journals and authored, co-authored or co-edited several books. He also oversees the interdisciplinary medical humanities minor at UNK. “The Cope Professorship recognizes a faculty member whose impact reaches every corner of UNK’s mission,” said Janet Eckerson, interim dean of the College of Arts and Sciences. “Dr. David Vail is a wonderful example of a faculty member whose excellence isn’t confined to one part of the job. He’s a nationally respected historian, an award-winning teacher, a leader in Nebraska’s humanities community and a supporter of first-generation students on campus.” Theodora Ziolkowski Ziolkowski, an assistant professor in the Department of English , was selected as the Paul W. Reynolds and Clarice Kingston Reynolds Chair of Creative Writing. Her five-year appointment includes a $10,000 annual stipend. As Reynolds Chair, she will organize the Reynolds Visiting Writers Series, which brings poets and authors to campus for public readings, talks and master classes. An accomplished fiction writer and poet, Ziolkowski teaches undergraduate and graduate creative writing courses at UNK, where she’s been a faculty member since 2022. She is the author of the Next Generation Indie Book Award-winning novella “On the Rocks” and the 2025 poetry collection “Ghostlit.” Her poetry, fiction and essays have appeared in more than 60 literary journals, anthologies and exhibits. In addition to her roles at UNK, she serves as a faculty mentor in the University of Nebraska at Omaha’s low-residency MFA program. Beissel Heath, chair of the Department of English, called Ziolkowski a “rising star” in the world of creative writing who’s highly regarded by her peers and colleagues. “She brings a fresh voice and an exuberant talent to the Reynolds Chair,” Beissel Heath said. “Theodora is distinguishing herself in the English Department as an outstanding advocate for the arts and humanities, a passionate teacher and generous mentor, and a gifted community builder inside and outside the classroom.” Michelle Beissel Heath Beissel Heath was selected as the Dave and Tancie Martin Chair in English. The three-year appointment provides $5,000 in total support. She also holds a Martin Distinguished Professorship and was recognized last week with the Pratt-Heins Award for Scholarship and Research. A UNK faculty member since 2009, Beissel Heath has established an international reputation through her research in Victorian and children’s literature. Her scholarly work includes peer-reviewed journal articles, book chapters, reviews and presentations at national and international conferences. Her 2018 book, “Nineteenth-Century Fictions of Childhood and the Politics of Play,” examines games and play in British and American children’s culture during the 19th century. She is currently writing a second book and contributing a chapter on games and 19th-century children’s literature to a multivolume history being published by Cambridge University Press. English professor Susan Honeyman highlighted Beissel Heath’s commitment to mentoring undergraduate and graduate research along with her contributions as a scholar. “Michelle has consistently gone beyond classroom teaching to mentor undergraduate and graduate research in SSRP, URF, conference presentations and thesis work,” Honeyman said. Honeyman also praised Beissel Heath’s archival research and success securing grants to support her scholarship, calling her work “integral to a growing subfield on games as socializing texts.” “She is one of the most important scholars to cite in the study of games, early readers and specifically James Barrie’s ‘Peter and Wendy.’” Facebook Twitter/X LinkedIn Tags: College of Arts and Sciences , David Vail , English , History , Janet Eckerson , Kearney , Michelle Beissel heath , Susan Honeyman , Theodora Ziolkowski , University of Nebraska at Kearney , UNK Post navigation From Nebraska to Alaska: UNK brings speech-language pathology education to students 3,300 miles away ‘It’s fun to be good’: Brian Alber, Tyler Hawk set the tone for UNK bands Search Search Submit News Submit story ideas for unknews.unk.edu Most Read Posts UNK launching new Loper Flight Academy with Thursday event Loper Flight Academy launches new era for UNK Aviation ‘A total win-win’: UNK program offers free counseling services for community members UNK College of Business and Technology recognizes Jerry Catlett with Distinguished Alumnus Award Vail, Ziolkowski, Beissel Heath selected for endowed faculty positions at UNK Photo Galleries Athletics Featured News Photo Galleries Trending PHOTOS: Cope Stadium comes alive for Loper football season opener against Wayne State August 27, 2026 gottulatm Events Featured News Photo Galleries Student Life PHOTOS: Loper Lot Party takes UNK students to beautiful Yanney Park August 27, 2026 Tyler Ellyson Featured News Photo Galleries Trending PHOTOS: Students fill classrooms and campus spaces for fall 2026 semester August 24, 2026 Erika Pritchard Events Featured News Photo Galleries Student Life PHOTOS: Blue Gold Welcome filled with friendly faces and fun activities August 23, 2026 Erika Pritchard Log in Copyright © 2026 UNK NEWS Theme by: Theme Horse Proudly Powered by: WordPress Facebook Twitter/X LinkedIn"
